# TypeScript and React

- Enable **strict** TypeScript; prefer **`interface`** for object shapes.
- Avoid **`any`**; use **`unknown`** and narrow at boundaries.
- For BFF/upstream responses, define **minimal** interfaces or narrow assertions where the contract is known — do not over-model entire upstream schemas unless maintained intentionally.
- **Functional components** and hooks only; no class components.

## `"use client"` placement

- Server Components are the **default** in App Router. Do not add `"use client"` unless the component needs browser APIs, event handlers, `useState`, `useEffect`, or TanStack Query hooks.
- Add `"use client"` at **leaf nodes** — the lowest component in the tree that actually needs client interactivity.
- **Never** mark a layout, a data-fetching wrapper, or a route segment as `"use client"` — this collapses the entire subtree into a client bundle and loses RSC benefits.
- If only a small part of a component needs interactivity, extract that part into a separate client component rather than marking the whole parent.

### Third-party UI barrels without `"use client"`

Some design-system packages ship a client-only barrel (hooks / `createContext`) **without** a `"use client"` directive. Importing that barrel from an RSC page or layout fails the Next.js build.

- Prefer a **host leaf wrapper** (thin client module that re-exports or composes the package) so route files stay Server Components.
- If a wrapper is not yet in place, mark the **smallest route entry** that imports the barrel — not the root layout and not a data-fetching wrapper.
- Document the exception in the portal spec or ADR.
- Do **not** copy package primitives into `components/ui` to dodge the RSC boundary.
